nodecode-enchant is a rewrite of noxius-studium's noxius-studium/enchanted-composer:

  https://github.com/noxius-studium/enchanted-composer
  commit b91e684c78d8a43a5346339a5091a1109424a28c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Composer Enhancements contributors (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the review half of the plugin, in Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell
contract. Its five enhancers (Clarify, Tighten, Grammar, Expand, Summarize) and
their instruction texts; the one-shot rewrite, where the enhancer's body is the
system message, the draft is the user message, and the answer is asked to be the
rewritten prompt alone; the 12000-character ceilings on the draft and on the
answer; the review that cuts a rewrite into change groups by a token-level
longest-common-subsequence diff, each group a character range of the original and
the text that replaces it, offered whole past 512 tokens a side or past 32
groups, and composed back into the draft only for the groups the person picks;
the history that keeps up to 30 revisions of a draft and moves back and forth
through them without spending another model call, refusing to move when the
draft no longer stands where the history left it. The desktop buttons, the
groups ticked in a sheet and the drag reorder are replaced by /enchant and three
tools; the section names the default enhancer, the history depth and the
completion ceiling, where the original's settings page held a provider, a model
and a thinking level per rewrite.

Left out: the live voice half - the WebRTC and WebSocket realtime adapters, the
Codex subscription lane through a child CLI, the audio device and microphone
lease, and the voice provider catalogue - because a cell runs no audio pipeline;
the prompt library of folders, favourites, tags, import and export, which a
sibling cell already gives users; the draft shelf; and every part that exists
only because the original draws into the Hermes desktop composer: the composer
bar, the settings page, the tooltips, the spinners and the responsive layout.
Nothing of the original's own code is bundled: it is all reimplemented against
Nodecode's contract, and the rewrite runs on the session's own auxiliary model
rather than on a model the plugin's settings page picks.
